    "<think>\nOkay, I need to find the indices of the longest increasing subsequence in a list. Let me think about how to approach this.\n\nFirst, I remember that the longest increasing subsequence (LIS) problem is a classic dynamic programming problem. But the question here is not just to find the length of the LIS, but to return the actual indices of the elements in that subsequence.\n\nSo, the standard approach for LIS is to use dynamic programming where for each element, we track the length of the LIS ending at that index. But how do I track the actual indices?\n\nLet me think. For each position i in the list, I can keep track of the length of the LIS ending at i. Then, for each element, I can check all previous elements j < i where arr[j] < arr[i], and see if the length of the LIS ending at j plus one is longer than the current length at i. If so, I update the length and also track the previous index.\n\nBut how to track the path? Because for each position, I need to know not just the length but also the previous index in the subsequence. So maybe I can have a dp array where dp[i] is the length of the LIS ending at i, and a prev array where prev[i] is the index of the previous element in the subsequence.\n\nLet me outline the steps:\n\n1. Initialize two arrays: dp and prev.\n2. For each i from 0 to n-1:\n   a. Initialize dp[i] to 1 (since the minimum length is 1 for each element).\n   b. For each j from 0 to i-1:\n      i. If arr[j] < arr[i], then check if dp[j] + 1 > dp[i]. If so, update dp[i] to dp[j] + 1 and set prev[i] = j.\n3. After filling the dp array, find the index i with the maximum dp[i]. Then backtrack from that index to find the path.\n\nBut wait, there might be multiple indices with the same maximum length. So I need to choose the one that gives the earliest occurrence, or maybe the one with the earliest index? Or maybe the one that gives the longest path. But the problem says \"longest increasing subsequence\", so there might be multiple possible sequences. However, the problem says to return the indices of a longest increasing subsequence. So perhaps any one of them is acceptable.\n\nBut how to handle when there are multiple",
